A descendant-shaped rule (the `descendant` field of the rule's key, which
the ppx computes: a combinator reaching for an unclassed subject: a bare
element type, `*`, or a pseudo-class chain with no class of its own) is
emitted BEFORE own-element rules (the first key of the "Ordered sheet" in
`generate.ml`), so a genuine specificity TIE resolves to the child's own
atom, since the later rule in a tie wins. No CSS layer is involved: real
specificity decides between rules of different specificity, whatever their
position, so an ancestor's rule that is genuinely MORE specific still wins
(the patterns below). The words `base` (unconditional) and `conditional`
(wrapped in an at-rule, or a pseudo-class on the atom's own compound) below
only describe a rule's context. A `[%styled.global]` rule is emitted before
every atom, see `global-first.t`.

The ancestor-wildcard tie: a wrapper's
descendant rule (`._in_j8vuse * { color }`, a bundle - no class of its own on
the subject, a bare `*`) and the span's own class (`._a_4esjgz { color }`)
are genuinely TIED: one class selector each ((0,1,0) - `*` and the wrapper's
own class both contribute nothing beyond the one class), so without
something to break the tie the browser applies whichever is textually LAST.

  $ cat > gbp.ml <<EOF
  > [@@@css ("._a_4esjgz{color:black;}", "_a_4esjgz", "0,1,0 own", "color", 1, false)]
  > [@@@css ("._in_j8vuse * {color:white;}", "_in_j8vuse", "0,1,0 & *", "color", 1, true)]
  > EOF

  $ styled-ppx.generate gbp.ml
  /* This file is generated by styled-ppx, do not edit manually */
  ._in_j8vuse * {color:white;}
  ._a_4esjgz{color:black;}

What the browser does now, and why: both rules are unconditional and
unlayered. The descendant-shaped wrapper rule is emitted FIRST (the first key
of the order), so on the (0,1,0) vs (0,1,0) tie the span's own `color:black`, being LATER,
wins - restoring "an element's own class beats an ancestor's blind reach for
`*`/an unclassed descendant" without a cascade layer.

A subject WITH its own class - `.tiptap` here, or an `.id-...` class a
`$(binding)` reference would resolve to - is not this shape: it targets a
specific, identified element, not "whatever happens to be under here", so it
is not reordered and stays at its definition position.

  $ cat > classed_descendant.ml <<EOF
  > [@@@css ("._a_wrap9k .tiptap{color:navy;}", "_a_wrap9k", "0,2,0 & .tiptap", "color", 1, false)]
  > EOF

  $ styled-ppx.generate classed_descendant.ml
  /* This file is generated by styled-ppx, do not edit manually */
  ._a_wrap9k .tiptap{color:navy;}

The `.list li { display: grid }` pattern. The wrapper's rule
(`._a_listwrap li`, one class + one type selector, specificity (0,1,1)) is
MORE specific than the li's own atom (`._a_liblock`, (0,1,0)) - real CSS
specificity, not stylesheet position, decides, so the wrapper wins
regardless of which one the sort emits first (unlike the tied
ancestor-wildcard case above).

  $ cat > list_li.ml <<EOF
  > [@@@css ("._a_listwrap li{display:grid;}", "_a_listwrap", "0,1,1 & li", "display", 1, true)]
  > [@@@css ("._a_liblock{display:block;}", "_a_liblock", "0,1,0 own", "display", 1, false)]
  > EOF

  $ styled-ppx.generate list_li.ml
  /* This file is generated by styled-ppx, do not edit manually */
  ._a_listwrap li{display:grid;}
  ._a_liblock{display:block;}

The `.field input { font-size: 16px }` pattern. Same shape as
`.list li` above - `._a_fieldwrap input` is (0,1,1), the input's own atom
`._a_inputfs` is (0,1,0) - the wrapper wins by specificity; both classified
`base`.

  $ cat > field_input.ml <<EOF
  > [@@@css ("._a_fieldwrap input{font-size:16px;}", "_a_fieldwrap", "0,1,1 & input", "font", 64, true)]
  > [@@@css ("._a_inputfs{font-size:14px;}", "_a_inputfs", "0,1,0 own", "font", 64, false)]
  > EOF

  $ styled-ppx.generate field_input.ml
  /* This file is generated by styled-ppx, do not edit manually */
  ._a_fieldwrap input{font-size:16px;}
  ._a_inputfs{font-size:14px;}

The `@media (min-width:1280px) { .menu > :last-child {
margin-right: 0 } }` pattern versus the item's own, unconditional `margin-right`
atom. The wrapper's rule is wrapped in `@media`, so it is classified
`conditional`; the item's own atom is unconditional, so it is `base`.
Neither is layered, so this IS a specificity question:
`._a_menuwrap > :last-child` is one class plus one pseudo-class, (0,1,1),
strictly more specific than the plain atom's (0,1,0) - the wrapper wins at
that breakpoint by specificity, as in plain CSS. The
ordered sheet puts the descendant-shaped rule first (see "Ordered sheet" in
`generate.ml`), which would give a genuine tie to the item's own atom, but
specificity alone already decides this pair.

  $ cat > media_menu.ml <<EOF
  > [@@@css ("@media (min-width:1280px){._a_menuwrap > :last-child{margin-right:0;}}", "_a_menuwrap", "0,2,0 & > :last-child", "margin", 4, true)]
  > [@@@css ("._a_marginright{margin-right:24px;}", "_a_marginright", "0,1,0 own", "margin", 4, false)]
  > EOF

  $ styled-ppx.generate media_menu.ml
  /* This file is generated by styled-ppx, do not edit manually */
  @media (min-width:1280px){._a_menuwrap > :last-child{margin-right:0;}}
  ._a_marginright{margin-right:24px;}

The `.sameLineBadge > :first-child:not(:last-child) { display:
inline }` pattern. The wrapper's rule is `.sameLineBadge` (one class) plus
`:first-child` plus `:not(:last-child)` (a negation pseudo-class's own
specificity is its argument's - here another pseudo-class), so three
pseudo-class-equivalents: specificity (0,3,0). The badge's own atom
(`._a_displaydesktop`) is (0,1,0). The wrapper is more specific, so it wins;
both unconditional, both classified `base`.

  $ cat > same_line_badge.ml <<EOF
  > [@@@css ("._a_badgewrap > :first-child:not(:last-child){display:inline;}", "_a_badgewrap", "0,3,0 & > :first-child:not(:last-child)", "display", 1, true)]
  > [@@@css ("._a_displaydesktop{display:block;}", "_a_displaydesktop", "0,1,0 own", "display", 1, false)]
  > EOF

  $ styled-ppx.generate same_line_badge.ml
  /* This file is generated by styled-ppx, do not edit manually */
  ._a_badgewrap > :first-child:not(:last-child){display:inline;}
  ._a_displaydesktop{display:block;}

The `sameLineBadge` pattern: the same shape as above, but the badge's own
override is narrower under `@media` instead of unconditional:
`._a_ancestor > :first-child:not(:last-child) { display: inline }`
(0,3,0, `base` - unconditional, no pseudo-class on the wrapper's OWN
compound) versus `@media (max-width:1000px) { ._a_badgeblock { display:
block } }` (0,1,0, `conditional`). Nothing is layered, so ordinary
specificity decides between them, exactly like the
`media_menu.ml`/`same_line_badge.ml` cases above - the only difference
from `media_menu.ml` is which SIDE is more specific.

  $ cat > same_line_badge_media.ml <<EOF
  > [@@@css ("._a_ancestor > :first-child:not(:last-child){display:inline;}", "_a_ancestor", "0,3,0 & > :first-child:not(:last-child)", "display", 1, true)]
  > [@@@css ("@media (max-width:1000px){._a_badgeblock{display:block;}}", "_a_badgeblock", "0,1,0 own", "display", 1, false)]
  > EOF

  $ styled-ppx.generate same_line_badge_media.ml
  /* This file is generated by styled-ppx, do not edit manually */
  ._a_ancestor > :first-child:not(:last-child){display:inline;}
  @media (max-width:1000px){._a_badgeblock{display:block;}}

What the browser does now, and why: specificity is compared directly -
(0,3,0) beats (0,1,0), whatever the viewport width and regardless of
which rule is textually later. The ancestor's `display:inline` wins even
under 1000px, where the media query matches.
